  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 PM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\n\nPattern Synopsis: A persistent ribbon of faster northwest upper-\nlevel flow will extend from western Canada into the northeast U.S. \nthrough the rest of the work week. A few impulses are forecast to \ntranslate through this mean flow pattern during the period. A \nstronger wave is forecast to dive into the Pacific Northwest later \nFriday, resulting in downstream heights rising across the eastern \nU.S. for the weekend. Some model disagreement exists in how quickly \nthis ridging breaks down early next week which brings some \nuncertainty to the local forecast after this weekend. Temperatures \nthrough the period are expected to be mostly above normal, with high \ntemperatures well above normal for the weekend (widespread readings \nin the 70s).\n\nForecast Details: As mentioned above, northwest upper-level flow is \nexpected locally through Friday. Impulses streaming through this \nmean flow and the WAA ahead of these impulses will bring a periodic \nshower risk during the period. However, a lack of moisture should \nkeep significant rainfall chances to a minimum. The deepest cyclonic \nflow and coolest mid-level temperatures within this flow regime \narrive for Thursday into Friday which will support at least a few \nheavier showers where they occur. Seasonably mild conditions are \nexpected through Friday for the most part, with Thursday and \nThursday night the coolest period.\n\nThe upcoming weekend is looking fantastic at this point, with \nunseasonably warm temperatures (highs in the 70s) and dry conditions \nanticipated. This will overlap nicely with the rapidly developing \nfall colors over northern Michigan.\n\nThe ridging over the eastern half of the U.S. starts breaking down \nafter the weekend, signaling the return of cooler temperatures and \nrain chances. Still a fair amount of model spread in how quickly the \npattern shifts again, so adjustments to the forecast to start the \nnext work week are likely.",
